.TH TREEHASH 1 "2026-09-08" "treehash 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
treehash \(em compute deterministic SHA-256 Merkle tree root hash and pin manifest
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B treehash
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ WORKSPACE_DIR ]
.P
.B treehash \-\-help
.P
.B treehash \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B treehash
is an intentionally small, auditable, dependency\-free ISO C17 command\-line
utility for Linux that recursively walks a workspace directory, respects
hierarchical
.B .gitignore
exclusion rules, hashes regular files deterministically using SHA\-256, and emits
a stable Merkle\-style root hash alongside a canonical SHA\-256 pin manifest.
.P
When
.I WORKSPACE_DIR
is omitted, it defaults to the current working directory
.RB ( . ).
.P
.B treehash
adheres strictly to the Unix philosophy: doing one job cleanly and predictably,
without hidden background services, daemons, IPC, telemetry, or external
runtime dependencies beyond standard ISO C17 libc and POSIX.1\-2008 system
interfaces.
It performs pure read\-only inspection and never alters files, timestamps, or
permissions.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage guidance to standard output and exit with status
.BR 0 .
Must be supplied as the sole argument; combining
.B \-\-help
with other options or operands is an error (exit status
.BR 2 ).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information
.RB ( "treehash 0.1.0" )
to standard output and exit with status
.BR 0 .
Must be supplied as the sole argument; combining
.B \-\-version
with other options or operands is an error (exit status
.BR 2 ).
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I WORKSPACE_DIR
Target root directory to inspect and hash.
If omitted, defaults to
.RB \(lq . \(rq
(the current working directory).
Specifying multiple directory operands, an empty string operand, or an unknown
option flag fails closed with exit status
.BR 2 .
Any attempt to specify a path containing relative directory traversal escapes
.RB ( .. )
is rejected fail\-closed as a path escape with exit status
.BR 2 .
.SH DIRECTORY TRAVERSAL
Directory traversal begins at
.I WORKSPACE_DIR
and navigates all accessible subdirectories recursively:
.IP \(bu 3
.B Unconditional .git/ Exclusion:
Any directory named
.B .git
is ignored unconditionally at the workspace root and at every nested
subdirectory level.
Traversal never descends into
.BR .git/ ,
preventing Git metadata volatility (such as index locks, pack updates, or reflogs)
from altering the workspace hash.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Deterministic Traversal Depth:
Directory recursion is bounded to a maximum depth of 256 levels
.RB ( TREEHASH_MAX_DEPTH ).
If this depth limit is reached, traversal terminates immediately fail\-closed
with diagnostic
.B treehash: RECURSION_DEPTH_EXCEEDED
and exit status
.BR 2 .
.IP \(bu 3
.B Ancestor Cycle Detection:
The walker tracks device and inode numbers
.RB ( st_dev ,
.BR st_ino )
of active ancestor directories up to the recursion depth limit.
If an ancestor directory is revisited (such as via recursive bind mounts or
directory hard link cycles), traversal terminates immediately fail\-closed with
diagnostic
.BI "treehash: CYCLE_DETECTED: \(dq" PATH \(dq
and exit status
.BR 2 .
.IP \(bu 3
.B Path Length Limits:
Constructed filesystem paths are bounded to 4096 bytes
.RB ( PATH_MAX ).
Paths exceeding this limit fail closed with diagnostic
.B treehash: PATH_LENGTH_EXCEEDED
and exit status
.BR 2 .
.SH IGNORE RULES (.GITIGNORE)
.B treehash
discovers and evaluates hierarchical
.B .gitignore
files found within the workspace:
.IP \(bu 3
As traversal enters a directory, any
.B .gitignore
file inside that directory is parsed and its rules are pushed onto an active
ignore stack tagged with the current directory depth.
.IP \(bu 3
When traversal exits a directory, all ignore rules associated with that depth
are popped and released.
.IP \(bu 3
Supported rule semantics:
.RS 3
.IP \(bu 3
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored as comments.
.IP \(bu 3
Trailing slashes (for example,
.BR build/ )
restrict matching strictly to directories.
.IP \(bu 3
Wildcards:
.B *
matches zero or more characters (except slashes),
.B ?
matches any single character (except slashes), and
.B [...]
matches character classes via
.BR fnmatch (3).
.IP \(bu 3
Leading slashes (for example,
.BR /dist )
anchor matching to the directory level where the enclosing
.B .gitignore
file resides.
.IP \(bu 3
Negation rules: Patterns prefixed with
.B !
re\-include previously excluded paths, allowing selective overrides.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Directory Pruning:
When an entire directory matches an exclusion rule, traversal skips descending
into that directory entirely, eliminating unnecessary I/O.
.RE
.SH SYMBOLIC LINKS AND SPECIAL FILES
To guard against infinite loops, out\-of\-tree escapes, and process blocking:
.IP \(bu 3
All filesystem inspections use
.BR lstat (2)
exclusively.
The
.BR stat (2)
system call is never invoked, ensuring symbolic links are never traversed or
resolved to their targets.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Symbolic Links:
Evaluated via
.BR S_ISLNK .
Symbolic links (whether pointing to files, directories, or nonexistent targets)
are never followed.
Directory symlinks are never recursed into, preventing symlink loops and workspace
boundary escapes.
Symbolic links are excluded from leaf hashing and do not appear in the pin manifest.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Special Files:
Character device nodes
.RB ( S_ISCHR ),
block device nodes
.RB ( S_ISBLK ),
named pipes / FIFOs
.RB ( S_ISFIFO ),
and UNIX domain sockets
.RB ( S_ISSOCK )
are identified via POSIX mode macros and are never opened with
.BR open (2)
or read.
This prevents process hangs and blocking I/O.
All special files are excluded from leaf hashing and the pin manifest.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Regular Files Only:
Only filesystem entries conforming to
.BR S_ISREG
have their contents read and hashed.
.SH PATH NORMALIZATION AND SORTING
.IP \(bu 3
.B Path Normalization:
All regular file paths are normalized relative to
.IR WORKSPACE_DIR .
Redundant leading
.B ./
prefixes are stripped, consecutive slashes
.RB ( // )
are collapsed into single slashes
.RB ( / ),
and paths are canonicalized without relative directory escapes
.RB ( .
or
.BR .. ).
.IP \(bu 3
.B Bytewise Lexicographic Sorting:
Directory enumeration order from
.BR readdir (3)
is treated as untrusted and filesystem\-dependent.
All eligible regular file entries are accumulated into memory and sorted in
strict bytewise lexicographic order using raw
.BR strcmp (3).
This ensures deterministic, locale\-independent, and platform\-reproducible
ordering across all Linux filesystems.
.SH CRYPTOGRAPHIC HASHING AND MERKLE TREE
.IP \(bu 3
.B Streaming SHA\-256:
File contents are read and hashed using an embedded FIPS 180\-4 SHA\-256
implementation in fixed 64\|KiB streaming chunks
.RB ( TREEHASH_IO_BUFFER_SIZE\ =\ 65536 ).
Files of arbitrary size are hashed in constant O(1) memory per file.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Leaf Digest Computation:
For each sorted regular file, its 32\-byte raw SHA\-256 content digest is formatted
as a 64\-character lowercase hexadecimal string.
The leaf record digest
.I H_leaf
is computed by hashing the canonical manifest line:
.RS 3
.P
.IR H_leaf\ =\  SHA\-256(file_hex\ +\ \(dq\ \ \(dq\ +\ rel_path\ +\ \(dq\en\(dq)
.RE
.IP \(bu 3
.B Binary Merkle Tree Reduction:
Sorted leaf digests form the base level (Level 0) of a binary Merkle tree:
.RS 3
.IP \(bu 3
Consecutive adjacent pairs
.RI ( H_2i ,
.IR H_2i+1 )
in raw 32\-byte binary form are concatenated (64 bytes total) and hashed:
.IR H_parent\ =\  SHA\-256( H_2i\  ||\  H_2i+1 ).
.IP \(bu 3
At any level with an odd number of nodes, the lone rightmost node is promoted
directly to the next level without duplicate hashing.
.IP \(bu 3
Iterative reduction continues level\-by\-level until a single 32\-byte binary root
digest is obtained, rendered as a 64\-character lowercase hexadecimal string.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Empty Workspace:
For an empty workspace containing zero eligible regular files, the root hash is
the canonical SHA\-256 digest of the empty string:
.BR e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855 .
.RE
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
Output is emitted strictly to standard output:
.P
.RS
.nf
ROOT <64\-hex\-root\-hash>
<64\-hex\-file\-hash>  <normalized\-relative\-path>
\&...
.fi
.RE
.P
The first line contains
.B ROOT
followed by a single space and the 64\-character lowercase hexadecimal Merkle
tree root hash.
.P
Each subsequent line represents one regular file, consisting of its 64\-character
lowercase hexadecimal SHA\-256 content digest, followed by exactly two ASCII
spaces
.RB ( \(dq\ \ \(dq ),
followed by its normalized relative path, terminated by a newline
.RB ( \en ).
Entries are printed in strict bytewise lexicographic order of relative paths.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Diagnostic messages are printed strictly to standard error.
All paths, arguments, and untrusted strings rendered to standard error are
sanitized:
non\-printable ASCII bytes (bytes less than 0x20 or greater than 0x7E),
backslashes
.RB ( \e ),
and double quotes
.RB ( \(dq )
are escaped to uppercase
.BI \ex HH
hexadecimal sequences, preventing ANSI terminal control sequence injection.
.P
Common diagnostic messages include:
.TP
.B treehash: unknown option '\fIopt\fR'
An unrecognized option flag was passed on the command line.
.TP
.B treehash: too many arguments; expected at most one workspace directory
More than one directory operand was provided.
.TP
.B treehash: invalid empty workspace directory
An empty string
.RB ( \(dq\(dq )
was passed as the workspace directory operand.
.TP
.BI "treehash: PATH_ESCAPE: \(dq" PATH "\(dq"
The directory operand contains relative traversal escape segments
.RB ( .. ).
.TP
.B treehash: PATH_LENGTH_EXCEEDED
A filesystem path exceeded 4096 bytes
.RB ( PATH_MAX ).
.TP
.B treehash: RECURSION_DEPTH_EXCEEDED
Directory traversal exceeded the maximum recursion depth limit of 256.
.TP
.BI "treehash: CYCLE_DETECTED: \(dq" PATH "\(dq"
A directory loop or cyclic filesystem structure was encountered.
.TP
.BI "treehash: cannot access '\fIPATH\fR': " REASON
The workspace directory does not exist or permission was denied.
.TP
.BI "treehash: '\fIPATH\fR': Not a directory"
The target workspace path is not a directory.
.TP
.BI "treehash: cannot read '\fIPATH\fR': " REASON
A file could not be opened or read during traversal.
.TP
.B treehash: OUT_OF_MEMORY
Heap memory allocation failed during execution.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success: the workspace was walked, regular files were hashed, and the root hash
and pin manifest were emitted to standard output; or a sole informational option
.RB ( \-\-help ,
.BR \-\-version )
was executed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Reserved for operational notice or hash mismatch in future verification modes.
.TP
.B 2
Operational or usage failure: command\-line syntax error, target path missing or
not a directory, permission denied, recursion depth or path length limit
exceeded, directory cycle detected, memory allocation failure, or standard
output write/flush failure.
.P
.B SIGPIPE
is ignored unconditionally via POSIX
.BR signal (2)
at startup.
If standard output is closed prematurely by a downstream consumer (such as
.BR head (1)),
the resulting pipe error surfaces as a checked stdio write failure with exit
status
.BR 2 .
.SH SECURITY AND LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 3
.B Metadata Immunity:
Only regular file byte content and relative path strings contribute to the
Merkle root hash and leaf digests.
File modification times, permission modes, ownership (UID/GID), inode numbers,
and extended attributes are not hashed, ensuring reproducible hashes across
clones and environments.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Excluded Objects:
Symbolic links, directory symlinks, character devices, block devices, named
pipes (FIFOs), and UNIX domain sockets are not hashed and do not appear in the
pin manifest.
.B treehash
is not a general\-purpose archival tool (such as
.BR tar (1)).
.IP \(bu 3
.B Non\-Goals:
.B treehash
is not a version control system and does not manage branches, commits, or
staging areas.
It does not perform remote networking, socket communication, or telemetry.
It does not run background daemons or filesystem watchers.
It does not write persistent index files, caches, or databases to disk.
It does not require elevated privileges
.RB ( sudo
or capabilities).
.SH EXAMPLES
Compute Merkle root hash and pin manifest for current directory:
.P
.RS
.nf
treehash
.fi
.RE
.P
Compute Merkle root hash and pin manifest for a specific workspace:
.P
.RS
.nf
treehash /path/to/project
.fi
.RE
.P
Save the manifest to a file for integrity auditing:
.P
.RS
.nf
treehash . > workspace.manifest
.fi
.RE
.P
Verify file contents using standard
.BR sha256sum (1):
.P
.RS
.nf
tail \-n +2 workspace.manifest | sha256sum \-c \-
.fi
.RE
.P
Display usage help:
.P
.RS
.nf
treehash \-\-help
.fi
.RE
.P
Display version information:
.P
.RS
.nf
treehash \-\-version
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sha256sum (1),
.BR git (1),
.BR fnmatch (3),
.BR lstat (2)
